@* Englische Fassung von Datenschutz.cshtml (Plan A9); die View-Lokalisierung wählt sie bei Sprache „en“. Inhalt gleich halten. *@
@{
    ViewData["Title"] = "Privacy";
}

<div class="container" style="max-width: 860px;">
    <h1 class="h3 fw-bold mb-2">Privacy</h1>
    <p class="text-muted">
        Which data the Citizen Portal processes – as of this student project. All persons in the BundID simulator are
        fictitious; please do not enter any real data.
    </p>

    <div class="card border-0 shadow-sm rounded-4 mb-3">
        <div class="card-body p-4">
            <h2 class="h5"><i class="bi bi-shield-check me-2" aria-hidden="true"></i>Signing in with BundID</h2>
            <p>
                When you sign in, BundID (here: the simulator) transmits your details to the portal: name, birth name, date
                and place of birth, address, email address, the trust level reached, the means of identification, a
                pseudonymous identifier (bPK2) and the mailbox handle (address of your BundID mailbox).
            </p>
            <p class="mb-1">Only what is needed to recognise you and to send you messages is <strong>stored
                permanently</strong>:</p>
            <ul>
                <li>bPK2 (pseudonymous identifier, valid for this portal only),</li>
                <li>mailbox handle,</li>
                <li>the trust level last reached and the time of the last sign-in.</li>
            </ul>
            <p class="mb-0">
                All other details are kept only in your session (encrypted cookie) and are discarded when you sign out or
                when the session ends. You can see exactly what was transmitted and stored under “My data”.
            </p>
        </div>
    </div>

    <div class="card border-0 shadow-sm rounded-4 mb-3">
        <div class="card-body p-4">
            <h2 class="h5"><i class="bi bi-hourglass-split me-2" aria-hidden="true"></i>Session and cookies</h2>
            <ul class="mb-0">
                <li>The portal's <strong>session cookie</strong>: ends after 30 minutes without activity, at the latest
                    8 hours after sign-in, or when you close the browser.</li>
                <li><strong>Sign-in cookies</strong> of the sign-in service (auth server) and of the BundID simulator
                    (sign-in session for the mailbox, 30 minutes).</li>
                <li><strong>Settings</strong>: language and colour scheme (cookie or the browser's local storage),
                    protection against forged forms (antiforgery cookie).</li>
                <li>No advertising or analytics cookies.</li>
            </ul>
        </div>
    </div>

    <div class="card border-0 shadow-sm rounded-4 mb-3">
        <div class="card-body p-4">
            <h2 class="h5"><i class="bi bi-database me-2" aria-hidden="true"></i>What you enter yourself</h2>
            <p class="mb-0">
                Appointments (service, location, time), applications (your details in the form), issue reports
                (description, location) and your settings are stored with your account. Confirmations are sent to your
                BundID mailbox (simulation) and stored there. No emails are sent.
            </p>
        </div>
    </div>

    <div class="card border-0 shadow-sm rounded-4 mb-3">
        <div class="card-body p-4">
            <h2 class="h5"><i class="bi bi-phone me-2" aria-hidden="true"></i>App on your smartphone</h2>
            <p>
                The installable app stores only program files (layout, scripts, images) on the device, no pages containing
                personal data. Without an internet connection only a notice appears.
            </p>
            <ul class="mb-0">
                <li>If you choose <strong>“Later”</strong> on the “Install app” notice on the start page, the browser stores
                    the time in local storage (<code>bpsim.pwa.hinweisSpaeter</code>) and does not show the notice for
                    30 days. The value never leaves the device.</li>
                <li>The portal generates the “Open on your phone” QR code itself; it contains only the portal's address.</li>
            </ul>
        </div>
    </div>

    <div class="card border-0 shadow-sm rounded-4 mb-3" id="benachrichtigungen">
        <div class="card-body p-4">
            <h2 class="h5"><i class="bi bi-bell me-2" aria-hidden="true"></i>Notifications on your phone</h2>
            <p>
                Only if you switch them on under Settings → Notifications. Your browser then creates a subscription with its
                vendor's push service (Chrome/Android: Google, Edge: Microsoft, Firefox: Mozilla, Safari/iPhone: Apple).
                The portal stores with your account:
            </p>
            <ul>
                <li>the address of this subscription at the push service and two keys of your browser for encryption,</li>
                <li>the time at which you switched notifications on.</li>
            </ul>
            <p>
                When a new message arrives in your BundID mailbox, the portal sends the title, subject and target (e.g.
                <code>/Postfach</code>) to your device via the push service – <strong>end-to-end encrypted</strong>
                (RFC 8291): the push service only sees that a message is coming for this device, not its content. You still
                read the text of the message only in the mailbox. The subject appears on the lock screen unless you have
                turned this off in your device settings.
            </p>
            <p class="mb-0">
                The setting applies per device. <strong>Notifications stay switched on on the device when you sign
                out</strong> – on a shared device, please switch them off first. If another person signs in on the device and
                switches notifications on, they will only go to that person. Switching off (or revoking the permission in the
                browser) deletes the subscription; subscriptions the push service no longer knows are deleted by the portal
                on the next send.
            </p>
        </div>
    </div>

    <div class="card border-0 shadow-sm rounded-4 mb-3">
        <div class="card-body p-4">
            <h2 class="h5"><i class="bi bi-globe2 me-2" aria-hidden="true"></i>External services</h2>
            <ul class="mb-0">
                <li><strong>Maps</strong> (“Discover the town”, issue reporting): map tiles from OpenStreetMap or CARTO, the
                    Leaflet map library via unpkg.com, address search via Nominatim (OpenStreetMap). These services receive
                    your IP address and the map section or search term.</li>
                <li><strong>Weather</strong>: the portal queries Open-Meteo with the town's coordinates – without any data
                    about you.</li>
                <li><strong>Google Maps</strong> opens only if you click the map link for a place.</li>
            </ul>
        </div>
    </div>

    <div class="card border-0 shadow-sm rounded-4">
        <div class="card-body p-4">
            <h2 class="h5"><i class="bi bi-hdd-network me-2" aria-hidden="true"></i>Operation</h2>
            <p class="mb-0">
                All services and the database run in containers on a virtual machine in Google Cloud (region
                us-central1, USA). Background of the project: <a asp-controller="Home" asp-action="Impressum">Legal notice</a>.
            </p>
        </div>
    </div>
</div>
